      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life of Commodus</strong><br>\nCommodus was born on August 31, 161 AD, as the son of the Roman Emperor Marcus Aurelius and his wife, Faustina the Younger. He was raised in the luxury of Rome's imperial family, with the expectation that he would one day become emperor. From an early age, Commodus received education in both academics and military skills, as was customary for someone of his status. His upbringing was marked by the Stoic philosophy that his father adhered to, although Commodus himself would later be known for rejecting these principles.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Ascension to Power</strong><br>\nCommodus became co-emperor with his father, Marcus Aurelius, in 177 AD when he was just 16 years old. This was part of Marcus Aurelius' plan to ensure a smooth succession. Commodus was with his father on the northern military campaigns when he was named Augustus. His rule began solely after his father's death in 180 AD, marking the first time a son succeeded his biological father since Titus succeeded Vespasian in 79 AD.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Reign as Emperor</strong><br>\nHis reign as emperor began with a period of relative peace and prosperity. However, Commodus soon became known for his erratic behavior and cruelty. He believed himself to be the reincarnation of Hercules and enjoyed participating in gladiatorial combat, an activity considered scandalous for an emperor. Commodus's focus on entertainment and self-indulgence led to neglect of the state's more pressing administrative and military issues.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
