      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Conspiracy and Attempted Assassination</strong><br>\nIn 182 AD, a conspiracy to assassinate Commodus was formed by members of the Senate and his own sister, Lucilla. The plan was uncovered, and the conspirators were executed. This event marked a turning point in Commodus's reign, making him increasingly paranoid and prompting a series of purges that eliminated perceived enemies and further destabilized his rule.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Economic Challenges</strong><br>\nUnder Commodus, Rome's economy began to suffer due to his lavish spending on games and the military. He devalued the Roman currency by decreasing the silver content in Roman denarii, causing inflation. This devaluation strained the economy and eroded the populace's confidence in the stability of the imperial government.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Administration and Governance</strong><br>\nCommodus's governance is noted for its reliance on favorites and the selling of public offices to the highest bidder. He was indifferent to the administration's finer details, often handing over power to unscrupulous officials who exploited their positions for personal gain. This led to widespread corruption and a general decline in the effectiveness of the imperial government.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
