            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Public Works and Building Projects</strong><br>\nDuring his reign, Commodus also invested in public works and building projects, including the renovation of the Colosseum after it was damaged by fire. He commissioned new structures to enhance his public image, including a massive statue of himself as Hercules. These projects were meant to win public favor and cement his divine status in the eyes of the people.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cultural and Religious Impact</strong><br>\nCommodus's belief in his divine status affected Rome's cultural and religious practices. He renamed Rome to Colonia Lucia Annia Commodiana and the months of the year after his own titles. This egocentric approach was met with ridicule and resistance, and many of these changes were reversed after his death.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nCommodus's reign came to a tumultuous end when he was assassinated on December 31, 192 AD. His assassination was part of a palace conspiracy involving his mistress and the Praetorian Guard. After his death, the Senate declared him a public enemy and restored the original names of the city and months. Commodus's death marked the end of the Antonine dynasty and ushered in a period of civil war and instability, known as the Year of the Five Emperors.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
